PROGRAM-CODE
// Define a constant to represent the total number of LEDs
#define numOfLed 5

// Create an array to store the Arduino pin numbers
// where the LEDs are connected (Pins 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6)
int ledPin[numOfLed] = {2, 3, 4, 5, 6};

// Define the delay time (500 milliseconds)
int wait = 500;

// The setup() function runs only once when the Arduino is powered ON or reset
void setup()
{
    // Use a for loop to configure all LED pins as OUTPUT
    for (int i = 0; i < numOfLed; i++)
    {
        pinMode(ledPin[i], OUTPUT);   // Set each LED pin as an output
    }
}

// The loop() function runs continuously
void loop()
{
    // ---------------- FORWARD DIRECTION ----------------

    // Turn ON LEDs one by one from LED1 to LED5
    for (int i = 0; i < numOfLed; i++)
    {
        digitalWrite(ledPin[i], HIGH); // Turn ON the current LED
        delay(wait);                   // Wait for 500 milliseconds
        digitalWrite(ledPin[i], LOW);  // Turn OFF the current LED
    }

    // ---------------- BACKWARD DIRECTION ----------------

    // Turn ON LEDs in reverse order from LED4 to LED2
    // (LED5 and LED1 are skipped to avoid repeating them)
    for (int i = numOfLed - 2; i > 0; i--)
    {
        digitalWrite(ledPin[i], HIGH); // Turn ON the current LED
        delay(wait);                   // Wait for 500 milliseconds
        digitalWrite(ledPin[i], LOW);  // Turn OFF the current LED
    }
}

Viva Questions
Q1. Which programming language is used in Arduino?
Answer: Arduino uses C++ (Arduino C++).
Q2. Why is an array used in this program?
Answer: The array stores the LED pin numbers, making it easy to control multiple LEDs using loops instead of writing separate code for each LED.
Q3. Why is a for loop used?
Answer: The for loop reduces code repetition and sequentially controls all the LEDs.
Q4. What is the purpose of delay(500)?
Answer: It pauses the program for 500 milliseconds, allowing each LED to remain ON for half a second.
Q5. Why does the backward loop start from numOfLed - 2?
Answer: It starts from LED4 (index 3) instead of LED5 to prevent LED5 from glowing twice consecutively, creating a smooth back-and-forth animation.

Can we change the pin numbers?
Yes. The Arduino Uno has 14 digital I/O pins (0–13), and most of them can be used to connect LEDs.
For example, if you want to use pins 7, 8, 9, 10, and 11 instead of 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6, simply change:
int ledPin[numOfLed] = {2, 3, 4, 5, 6};
to
int ledPin[numOfLed] = {7, 8, 9, 10, 11};
Then connect the LEDs to pins 7–11.
